## Doing the same work twice

<At levels="simple">

The chat in <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" /> re-sent the whole conversation on every call, and the model read it all again.
Even within one reply, it re-reads everything before each new token (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />). Does it really redo
all that work every time?

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The chat in <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" /> re-sent the whole conversation on every call: 163 tokens after the
first exchange, and 633 by the time you asked your question.
Inside each call, the token loop from <ChapterRef to="generating-text" /> does the same: to pick each new token, the model reads
everything before it again. Does it really redo all that work every time?

</At>

<At levels="simple">

It doesn't have to. In attention (<ChapterRef to="attention" />), each token makes a key and a value that depend only on the
text before it, so they never change once worked out. The model can store them and reuse them:
this store is the <GlossaryPopover term="kv-cache">KV cache</GlossaryPopover>. Step through GPT-2
small finishing "The cat sat on the" with the cache off and on:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

It doesn't have to. Remember how attention works (<ChapterRef to="attention" />): every token makes a query, a key, and a
value. Each token can only look at the tokens before it, so its key and value depend only on the
text before it, and adding more text later doesn't change them. The model can work them out once
and keep them. This store is the <GlossaryPopover term="kv-cache">KV cache</GlossaryPopover>: with
it, each new token only computes its own key and value, and its query is matched against the stored
ones. Step through GPT-2 small finishing "The cat sat on the" with the cache off and on:

</At>

<KVCacheStepper />

<At levels="simple">

Without the cache, each new token took longer than the last, and 500 tokens took 43.0 seconds.
With it, each took about the same time, about 20 milliseconds, and the run took 10.0 seconds. Both
runs wrote exactly the same text.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Without the cache, every step runs the whole text through all 12 blocks again, so each step costs
a little more than the last. Measured on a laptop, the time per new token grew from 23 to 139
milliseconds over 500 tokens, and the whole run took 43.0 seconds. With the cache, each token took
about 20 milliseconds, and the run took 10.0 seconds. Both runs picked exactly the same 500 tokens;
their scores differed by at most 0.01, from rounding when the same sums are added up in a different
order.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Counting the work

Without the cache, step $t$ (counting from 0) runs all $p + t$ tokens through the model, so $n$ new
tokens after a $p$-token prompt take

$$\sum_{t=0}^{n-1} (p + t) = n\,p + \frac{n(n-1)}{2}$$

token passes. For the 5-token prompt and 500 tokens here, that's 127,250, against 504 with the
cache: 252 times as many. Yet the run took only 4.3 times as long. Running one token through the
model is mostly waiting for its weights to arrive from memory, and many tokens in the same pass
share that trip, so the extra tokens are cheap.<Cite id="pope2022" /> With the cache, each step
still compares its query with every stored key, so the time per token crept up a little, from
about 19 to 20 milliseconds.

</At>

The cache works with the guess-and-check trick from <ChapterRef to="speculative-decoding" /> too: the text before the guesses already
has its keys and values stored, so the big model's checking pass only computes them for the guesses.

<At levels="simple">

The price is memory: GPT-2 small stores 18,432 numbers for every token, and for large models and
long prompts the cache runs to gigabytes.<Cite id="pope2022" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The price is memory. GPT-2 small stores a key and a value in each of its 12 blocks for every token:
2 × 12 × 768 = 18,432 numbers per token. For large models and long prompts the cache runs to
gigabytes, and keeping it small is a big part of serving them efficiently.<Cite id="pope2022" /> Some
other kinds of models avoid it by keeping a fixed-size memory instead.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The cache for a bigger model

Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, the model in the Harnesses chapters, has 28 blocks, and its attention has 12
heads of 128 numbers each. A key and a value for every head would be
2 × 28 × 12 × 128 = 86,016 numbers per token. It stores far fewer, because its 12 heads share 2
sets of keys and values, an arrangement called grouped-query attention:
2 × 28 × 2 × 128 = 14,336 numbers, six times fewer.<Cite id="qwen2024" /> At 2 bytes each, that's
about 29 KB per token, and about 0.94 GB for a full 32,768-token context.

</At>

## Caching across calls

<At levels="simple">

The cache for one reply is thrown away when the reply is done. But a chat's next call starts with
the same text as the last, plus a little more. So model providers keep the cache for the start
of a prompt for a while, and reuse it when a new prompt starts the same way. This is <GlossaryPopover term="prompt-caching">prompt caching</GlossaryPopover>,
and it makes those tokens much cheaper.<Cite id="openai-prompt-caching" /><Cite id="anthropic-prompt-caching" />
Work that can wait is cheaper too: Anthropic charges half price for requests sent in a batch and
answered later instead of straight away.<Cite id="anthropic-pricing" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The KV cache for one reply is thrown away when the reply is done. But a chat's next call starts
with exactly the same text, plus a little more. So model providers keep the cache for the start of
a prompt for a while, from minutes to hours, and a later prompt that starts the same way, token for
token, picks up from there. This is <GlossaryPopover term="prompt-caching">prompt caching</GlossaryPopover>.
Providers charge a tenth of the normal price or less for tokens read from the cache, and the reply
starts sooner, because there is less to work through first.<Cite id="openai-prompt-caching" /><Cite id="anthropic-prompt-caching" />
Cached tokens still count toward the context window, though: caching changes what they cost, not
how much room they take.<Cite id="anthropic-context-windows" /> Work that can wait is cheaper too:
Anthropic's Batch API, which answers requests later instead of straight away, charges half price
for both input and output tokens.<Cite id="anthropic-pricing" />

</At>

<At levels="simple">

The match has to be exact, from the very first token: change one early word and everything after
it has to be worked out again. See what that means for the <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" /> session:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The match has to be exact, from the very first token. Change one early word and every key and value
after it is different, because each depends on all the text before it. So the cache only helps up
to the first difference. See what that means for the <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" /> session:

</At>

<PromptCacheView />

- **Only appending** reuses 2,342 of the 2,975 tokens sent (79%). Each call only pays full price for
  what's new.
- **Putting the time at the top** of the system prompt makes every prompt different from its first
  line, so nothing is reused.
- **Editing the system prompt** throws the cache away once; later calls build a new one.
- **Dropping the oldest turn** (<ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />) sends fewer tokens, 2,189, but costs more than keeping them
  all: every drop changes the text right after the system prompt, so little else matches.
- **Summarizing** sends the fewest tokens and caches well between summaries, so it costs the least.

<At levels="deep">

With cached tokens at a tenth of the price, a call costs about $\text{new} + 0.1 \times \text{cached}$
in full-price tokens. Only appending sends 2,975 tokens, 2,342 of them cached, so it costs about
633 + 234 = 867: 29% of the uncached price. Putting the time at the top costs all 2,975.

</At>

That is why harnesses keep the parts that don't change at the front, in the same order every time:
tool definitions, then the system prompt, then the conversation. Anything that changes, like the
time or the new message, goes at the end, and the conversation only grows. The providers' own
guides give the same advice.<Cite id="openai-prompt-caching" />

The cache is not memory. It holds the model's working for text that is still in the prompt, so the
model knows nothing more than it did (<ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />), and it is deleted after a while without use. It
doesn't change the answer either: a cached prompt gets the same kind of reply as an uncached one.

Simplified: providers only cache prompts above a minimum length, from a few hundred to a few
thousand tokens depending on the model, and charge a little extra to write the cache. This small
session would be too short, so the view above ignores that limit.

<RecapCard>

- A token's keys and values depend only on the text before it, so the model can store them: the
  KV cache.
- With the cache, each new token costs about the same; without it, each costs more than the last.
  The answer is the same either way.
- Providers keep the cache for the exact start of a prompt, so harnesses put what doesn't change
  first and only ever append.

<At levels="deep">

- Without the cache, $n$ tokens take $np + n(n-1)/2$ passes; sharing keys and values across heads
  shrinks the cache.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** so far one model has answered everything. Apps often have several, cheap and
expensive. Who decides which one answers?
